func=jag::MainLogicManager::MouseInputRecorder::OnMouseMove	func_addr=0x00cbc920	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MainLogicManager::MouseInputRecorder::OnMouseMove -- THE PRODUCER OF THE MOVEMENT RING.\n\nCADENCE: ONCE PER OS MOUSE-MOTION EVENT, not once per frame. Evidence chain, all in this build:\n  (1) jag::MainLogicManager::Construct registers eight eastl::function delegates into the\n      InputHandler that Input holds as its global handler. The seventh of those registrations\n      targets THIS function and lands in the handler's onMouseMove listener list.\n  (2) jag::input::Input::OnMouseMotion walks exactly that listener list (same field, proven by\n      the named-handler loop in that function reading the same member) and finally invokes the\n      global handler's onMouseMove listeners with the event's (x,y).\n  (3) Input::OnMouseMotion is called straight out of the platform mouse-motion event handler,\n      once per delivered event. So a burst of N OS motion events in one frame pushes N samples,\n      each carrying its own event-time timestamp.\nTherefore the server does see intra-frame motion detail: bursts arrive in the ring with\nnear-identical timestamps and are all eligible for the wire.\n\nUNCONDITIONAL PUSH: there is no "has the cursor actually moved" test here. Every invocation\nwrites a slot. Duplicate positions are suppressed later, by the prot sender, not here.\n\nWRITES per invocation:\n  recorder->lastMoveRawX/Y      \e event coordinates as delivered\n  recorder->lastMoveScaledX/Y   \e the same coordinates after the DPI/UI scale transform\n  moveRing[write].button        \e -1  (marks "movement", distinguishing it from a press sample)\n  moveRing[write].rawX/rawY     \e unscaled event coordinates  <-- THESE are what the wire carries\n  moveRing[write].scaledX/scaledY \e scaled coordinates (local use only)\n  moveRing[write].timestamp     \e global monotonic ms clock, sampled HERE, i.e. at event time\nOVERFLOW: write advances then wraps at the slot count; if it then equals read, read is advanced\none step as well, i.e. drop-oldest, silently. The ring never blocks and never reports loss.\n\nRETURNS true (event handled) -- note this does NOT stop later listeners in Input's dispatch,\nwhich only short-circuits on the named-handler path.\n\nSIDE-EFFECT WARNING for synthetic injection: this ring is NOT private to the prot layer. The\ninterface/component logic peeks the newest entry (write-1, wrapping) every tick to obtain the\ncurrent cursor position. Writing entries here therefore moves the local UI hover/cursor notion.\nSee the peek site inside the large interface-logic function.\n\nWindows counterpart: the Win32 build has a SECOND, independent recorder for the native mouse prot\nwhose ring is fed by jag::ClientProt::RecordNativeMouseMove, bound to the low-level Win32 mouse\nmessage dispatch and carrying a per-sample source-flags byte. That is a different producer on a\ndifferent event; this function is the game-event path and exists on both platforms.\n\nName provenance: descriptive project coinage, not a Jagex symbol.
