func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001afd60	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Patcher target: MOV R8D, 0x50 (port 80 hardcoded for LIVE mode). The Project X patcher overwrites the 4-byte immediate at offset +2 with PROJECTX_HTTP_PORT. 948 pattern: 41 b8 50 00 00 00 74 (JZ follows). GetHTTPURL is NOT inlined into callers in 948 — this is the single site.
func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001afd60	kind=plate	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Setting prototype: eastl_fixed_string_256 * GetHTTPURL(eastl_fixed_string_256 * result, WorldLobbyData * this)
func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001afd60	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=147	cu_index_from_entry=33	text=Project X patcher target: the live-mode HTTP content port literal. This port is used when the ClientParameters TLS flag is clear and the server profile is the live/default one; the runtime patcher rewrites this immediate with the port from PROJECTX_HTTP_PORT so the client fetches JS5-over-HTTP content from the local server.\n\nHow the patcher locates it: by the pair of world-port base constants that this same basic block adds to the world id, taking the literal from its fixed position relative to them. It deliberately does NOT key on the surrounding opcode bytes, because register allocation around the literal differs between compilers, between builds, and between the two copies of this logic in this build.\n\nThis logic exists at MORE THAN ONE SITE in this build. The compiler also inlined the whole body of this function into jag::ClientCrashReportingDataProvider::GetAddress, which reaches the same WorldLobbyData through the Client. Both sites must be patched, or the crash-reporting path keeps reporting the unpatched port. Re-check the site count on every build and on every platform rather than assuming any particular shape.
func=jag::MainLogicManager::KeyboardInputRecorder::OnKeyDown	func_addr=0x00caf9a0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=⛔ TRAP: the keyboard rings use the OPPOSITE cursor convention from the mouse rings.\n\nThis producer indexes and advances the SECOND dword after the slot array, and bumps the FIRST one when the\ntwo collide (drop-oldest). So for the keyboard rings: first \e READ, second \e WRITE. Both mouse rings are the\nother way round - first \e WRITE, second \e READ - which was verified independently in SendEventMouseClick,\nPopEventMouseMoveSample and the movement producer. Anything writing these rings must not assume one\nconvention from the other.\n\nRecord layout confirmed here by the stores: keyId, charCode, rawKeyCode, repeat (byte), modifierMask,\ntimestamp. The timestamp is loaded from the same global monotonic clock the mouse movement producer uses,\nwhich independently corroborates that global.\n\nModifier folding: left/right variants of each modifier collapse onto one bit before being OR-ed into both the\nlive and the sticky mask. The repeat flag is set only when the key was already held AND the modifier mask is\nunchanged AND it is the same key as last time; it is a send filter only and never reaches the wire.
