func=jag::LoginManager::LoginStepWaitingServerClientVarLength	func_addr=0x00189560	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=LoginStepWaitingServerClientVarLength (login state 0xfa\e250) — reads the length prefix of a server-perm VarClient (varc) block (TASK B "Part A"). Waits for 2 bytes, reads gT_unsigned_short \e 2-byte BIG-ENDIAN block length -> loginMgr+0x1c8 (svrClientVarLen). Transitions to state 0x104 (block reader). The full Part-A framing is: [g2 length][block]; a block may be followed by more [g2 length][block] pairs until a block whose byte[0] continuation flag \e\e 1.
func=jag::Client::OpenErrorGamePage	func_addr=0x00b87320	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Builds the user-facing error page URL "http://world2.runescape.com/error_game_%s.ws" (or "%s/error_game_%s.ws" with configured host) and opens it (FUN_00ae4380) on a session/login error, then shuts the client to the error state. This is a USER-FACING browser redirect, NOT crash telemetry - it does not POST a backtrace or identify modules. Lower priority for suppression; only relevant if a forced error transition would expose our injection state. Distinct from nxtclienterror.ws (ReportError).
func=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct	func_addr=0x0007bb3e	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct -- constructs the MainLogicManager that the Client holds a\npointer to, and, critically, WIRES UP BOTH INPUT RECORDERS.\n\nIt contains two sub-objects, each of which stores the InputHandler pointer (Input's global\nhandler) in its own field 0 and then registers eastl::function delegates on that handler:\n\n  KeyboardInputRecorder (first sub-object): three delegates -- key down, key up, key char.\n  Layout it initialises: the per-key held-flag array, the held/distinct counters, a 255-record\n  key-PRESS ring with its cursor pair, then a 255-record typed-character ring with its cursor\n  pair. The character ring's cursor pair ends exactly where the mouse recorder begins, which is\n  the independent confirmation of both ring extents.\n\n  MouseInputRecorder (second sub-object): eight delegates in this registration order --\n  button0 down, button0 up, button1 down, button1 up, button2 down, button2 up, MOUSE MOVE,\n  wheel. The seventh lands in the handler's onMouseMove list, which is the same list\n  jag::input::Input::OnMouseMotion dispatches; that is the proof that movement samples are\n  produced per OS event. Layout it initialises: a 50-record press ring + cursor pair, then a\n  200-record movement ring + cursor pair, each ring loop followed by an 8-byte store clearing\n  exactly that ring's own cursors.\n\nEverything a synthetic-input producer needs to address lives in these two sub-objects.\n\nName provenance: matches the prior work on the Win32 build; the class name is this project's\nestablished coinage for the object the Client holds here.
func=jag::input::Input::OnScrollWheel	func_addr=0x00c93420	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::input::Input::OnScrollWheel(Input *this, float wheelX, float wheelY). Reference symbol: jag::input::Input::OnMouseWheel(float,float,float,float).\nThe listener signature is bool(float x, float y, float wheelX, float wheelY) where x/y are NOT arguments - they are read from this->lastMouseX (+0x88) / lastMouseY (+0x8C), which only OnMouseMotion writes. Dispatches handler->onMouseWheel (+0x1C8, lock +0x1E0).\nConsequence for injection: move the mouse first, or the wheel event carries a stale position.
